text:
Jane L.: quick question — I’m adding an IRSA role for the payments service and need to wire KMS access. I tried granting the role via aws_kms_grant but CI failed with AccessDenied when the pod tries to decrypt. Any quick guesses?

Alex M.: can you paste the trust policy + kms snippet? often it's either the role arn mismatch or key policy not allowing the role.

Jane L.: sure. terraform snippets from my branch:```hcl
resource "aws_iam_role" "payments_irsa" {
  name = "payments-irsa-role"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [{
      Action = "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity"
      Effect = "Allow"
      Principal = { Federated = aws_iam_openid_connect_provider.oidc.arn }
      Condition = { StringEquals = { "oidc.eks.amazonaws.com/id/EXAMPLED1234:sub" = "system:serviceaccount:payments:payments-sa" } }
    }]
  })
}

resource "aws_kms_key" "payments_key" {
  description = "payments data key"
  policy = jsonencode(local.kms_policy)
}

resource "aws_kms_grant" "payments_role_grant" {
  key_id = aws_kms_key.payments_key.key_id
  grantee_principal = aws_iam_role.payments_irsa.arn
  operations = ["Decrypt", "Encrypt"]
}
```

Jane L.: CI error log: `AccessDeniedException: User: arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/payments-irsa-role/web-identity-session is not authorized to perform: kms:Decrypt on resource`

Sanjay P.: two things to check: 1) key policy must allow the role (kms grants are ephemeral and can be tricky across accounts), 2) ensure the assumed-role ARN in runtime equals the role arn you granted — the assumed principal shows `assumed-role/.../web-identity-session` which is expected but the key policy needs to allow the role arn (not assumed-role session).

Alex M.: +1. If the key policy is restrictive, adding an explicit statement for \"AWS\": \"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/payments-irsa-role\" is required. Example local.kms_policy snippet:```json
{"Version":"2012-10-17","Statement":[{"Sid":"AllowUseByPaymentsRole","Effect":"Allow","Principal":{"AWS":"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/payments-irsa-role"},"Action":["kms:Decrypt","kms:Encrypt","kms:GenerateDataKey*"],"Resource":"*"}]}
```

Kevin T.: if you're using grants (aws_kms_grant) prefer granting to the role's arn (grantee_principal = aws_iam_role...arn) but remember grants can be removed if key rotation occurs; key policy permanent statement is more robust for long-term service accounts. For EKS/IRSA, also confirm the OIDC provider id in the assume_policy matches the account-level provider string exactly — mismatches (trailing / or wrong id) are common.

Jane L.: hmm, my key policy delegates administration to root only. I tried adding the grant because I thought it would be sufficient. I'll push a change adding a policy statement. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/infra/pull/412 (draft)

Alex M.: quick review: two small suggestions — 1) use interpolation so role arn is generated not hardcoded, e.g. `${aws_iam_role.payments_irsa.arn}`, 2) add a condition to the key policy to limit principal via `StringEquals":{"aws:PrincipalArn":"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/payments-irsa-role"}` if you want extra safety.

Sanjay P.: for multi-account case: if KMS in central security account and EKS in app account, you need cross-account principal in the key policy: `arn:aws:iam::APP_ACCOUNT:role/payments-irsa-role` and also ensure the role can be assumed across accounts.

Kevin T.: small terraform tip: put the policy JSON into local.kms_policy using templatefile or jsonencode to keep it readable. Also add `depends_on = [aws_iam_role.payments_irsa]` to the grant resource to avoid race.

Jane L.: pushed update: added policy statement and switched to key policy allowance instead of relying on grant. infra-bot: ci status?

infra-bot: CI — plan & apply checks passed for infra/payments (branch: jane/payment-kms-irsa) :white_check_mark:

Jane L.: redeployed pod, decrypt succeeded. closing loop — thanks for the pointers :raised_hands: If anyone wants I can open a mini-doc in docs/infra about IRSA + KMS gotchas.

Alex M.: please do, a short checklist would be great.

Sanjay P.: would help ops too.

Kevin T.: nice work!
